title The flag inside a longer word, and two guards nobody ran
prompt Autonomous loop -- pull xykj61 first, roster cold, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, send each finished increment
think Opened on a tree the previous lap had staged and never committed: nineteen paths in the index, no commit. The runner refuses a cold full-roster pass over a dirty index under lap_unclosed, which is exactly right and exactly the state -- so the honest first move was to close that lap rather than route around its refusal.
think Ran the roster --hot over the staged tree instead. 67 of 67 green, tree_moved=no, so the package was verified against the tree it would ship and I committed it as d1d1248d46. Then the round-open pull, which was up to date. N moved 3488 -> 3489, so the rota row moved 3 -> 4 rather than repeating -- the previous lap had read 3 and made no commit, which is the measured skew the seed names.
think Council rota row 4 -- Earth, Mercury, the concrete, the sense aroma. Read the threshold and all three seats. The cardinal seat (the one-clock naming law) and the fixed seat (Two Rooms) both say the same thing about a door: the fact is read at the door, before interpretation. The dual seat (the marked value) says a value crossing a seam wears a mark naming its kind. Every finding this round turned out to be a boundary read wrong at a door.
think Standfast first: %230, %252, %255 are all Keaton's, and %248 holds the integration. Nothing agent-closable stood open, so the crux was the previous lap's recommend -- four advisory dialect families the same meter already prints.
think The card said grep -P 6, readlink -f 5, sed -i 5, stat -c 2 -- eighteen. Listing every site file by file rather than trusting the count read TWELVE real ones, and stat -c read ZERO: its one site was already a portable pair. Measurement beats memory, including a measurement taken one lap ago, which is REDS %257's own lesson arriving again one week later.
think Chose the crux Lindy-first: grep -P, four sites, standing in the two guards this tree leans on hardest. PCRE's (?<![A-Za-z0-9_.-]) has no ERE spelling, yet the rule does -- CONSUME the boundary character and strip it. Under -o the two are identical, since a match may begin only where the character before it sits outside the class, which is what the lookbehind asked. That is strictly better than a perl fallback: a fallback means the second pier runs a different code path, and a path only one host runs is a path nobody checks.
think Proved the equivalence on the whole tree rather than on fixtures. 35,642 references under GNU grep, sorted byte-identical between the two spellings, and the census reads the same ten numbers before and after. The living card scan reads the same nineteen lines. A third pattern -- the asserted-absent subtraction -- had a lookbehind that could never refuse anything, since ! -[fe] + ends on a space and a space is already outside the class; dropping it changes no reading.
think The pattern stood spelled out three times in one file, which is one rule three copies could come to disagree about in the one place where disagreeing means miscounting every dated reference in the tree. Hoisted to DP_REF_BODY, used three ways.
think Then the first red. tools/fixtures/ryekey_control.sh read a build mtime BSD-first: stat -f %Fm || stat -c %.Y. GNU reads -f as --file-system, so the first leg asked for the filesystem holding a file named %Fm, which fails, AND the one holding the binary, which succeeds -- five lines to stdout, exit 1, and the || then appending the mtime underneath. Six lines where one was meant, on every GNU run. Its comparisons passed anyway, because the five-line prefix was constant. The rule against exactly this was written one lap earlier, inside the date functions' own comment, where no stat author would pass. Booked %260, moved to file_mtime in shell_portable.sh where the next dialect question will find it.
think Second red, and it is the pattern reading its own boundary wrong. The grep family matched grep[[:space:]]+...-P anywhere in a line, so pgrep -P -- a parent process id, spelled identically on both piers -- counted as a GNU-only site. Six where four lines carry it. The census beside it learned a week ago that a reference begins at a boundary; the meter hunting for dialect had never applied it to itself. Booked %261. Named the limit rather than hiding it: zgrep -P and xzgrep -P are read past too, and nothing here writes either.
think Third red, and it is mine. Testing whether a C-locale byte range could replace grep -P, the reading came back zero on a file plainly carrying high bytes, and my first reading of that was GNU grep refusing a bracket range. It was not GNU grep. `type grep` says this bench's shell defines grep as a FUNCTION wrapping ugrep 7.8.4, while `sh -c 'command -v grep'` answers /run/current-system/sw/bin/grep, GNU 3.12 -- exactly what the scan's own comment already claimed. A whole-tree parity check taken the wrong way read 26,817 where GNU reads 35,642. Booked %262: measure through the shell the subject runs in. This is REDS %174's family reaching the bench rather than the index.
think Fourth red, and the one that mattered most. Running ryekey_control.sh after repairing its btime line -- because it was open, which is luck -- it read RED: fresh build left no receipt. It had been red for a day and stood on none of seventy roster rows. Root: rye/bin/rye is untracked and gitignored, this pier's copy was built 20260821, and the receipt feature landed in rye/src/main.rye on 20260825. Nothing in the tree was wrong, and a RED meaning "your binary is stale" reads exactly like "the feature is broken."
think Repaired it three ways rather than one. Rebuilt from tracked source (sh rye/bootstrap.sh, one second) and all fifteen legs went green in nineteen. Taught the control to ask the binary its version at the door and compare it against what rye/src/main.rye declares -- one-clock stamps, which string-compare correctly -- so a binary standing behind its own source SKIPS at exit 0 naming the rebuild. Gated where the requirement is known rather than where it is discovered. And wrote tools/r/ryekey_witness.rish so a lap actually runs it: roster 70 -> 71.
think The version read had its own small lesson: rye version reports through std.debug.print, which is stderr. Asking the stream rather than assuming it took one command; guessing would have shipped a control that skipped forever on a healthy binary.
think Fifth red, found by reading the diff rather than the verdict. reds_ledger_headline_write.sh rewrote the ledger's total, remainder and span and left the stamp saying WHEN they were counted. The numbers moved 259 -> 263 while the sentence still claimed 20260826.070513, two hours and four rows earlier. Both changed=yes and verdict=ok were true of a rewrite that leaves a date behind. Booked %264: a figure carries a unit, a date, and a source, and a generator that refreshes the number owns the date beside it.
obs Four of the five reds are one sentence at different scales: a rule this tree had already written, standing somewhere the next author would not pass. The stat order was in the date comment. The boundary rule was in the census. The one that was NOT that shape -- the off-roster control -- is the one that had actually been failing.
obs Every gated family is proven from both sides on a planted tree, and three of four now by behaviour: a real guard reading the same number under both dialects while a planted elder copy still breaks. The PCRE leg borrows dated_path_control.sh's throwaway tree, which runs the same scan in about a second where the field takes forty-five, and adds the two shapes the boundary rule exists for -- so its answer is WANTED at two rather than merely observed. Proven from the refusing side by hand: drop the boundary group and the same pen reads three.
obs The stat leg needs no shim to show the fault, because the fault is what a GNU host does today. It reproduces the six-line answer directly and then proves the repaired order under a stat that refuses -c outright. An order proven only on the host it was written for is an order nobody checked.
obs Held the integration gate. %248 reserved the two-pier merge for Keaton's word; main stays unpushed with four packages now parked additively behind it.
